      "keyInsight": "The people most likely to suffer from crippling, mysterious pain are not lazy slackers. They are the reliable rock stars of everyday life: perfectionists, overachievers, and compulsive caretakers. These are the folks who never say no, always clean the dishes, meet every deadline, and smile through chaos. On the surface, they look like model citizens. But deep in their emotional basement, a furious pressure cooker is roaring. Being responsible, dutiful, and selfless round the clock takes huge mental energy. Inside every agreeable adult lives a wild, exhausted animal shouting that it wants to quit and walk away. That dark rage feels shameful to a good person. To keep that fiery tantrum from bursting out, the brain clamps down on muscles with a vice grip of physical pain.",
      "content": "Think of your mind like a busy home kitchen during Thanksgiving dinner. Upstairs in the dining room, you are the perfect host. You smile warmly, pass the dishes, fold every napkin neatly, and apologize whenever someone bumps into you. You want everyone pleased and calm. Psychologists call this trait conscientiousness or perfectionism. You set sky-high standards for yourself. You carry the family burdens, balance the budget, and never let anyone down. You pride yourself on being dependable, patient, and completely in control.\n\nNow look down at the kitchen floorboards. Deep in the basement underneath that neat party, a furnace is burning red-hot. That basement holds raw, primal, selfish feelings. It hates endless chores. It hates smiling when exhausted. It hates pleasing demanding bosses and difficult relatives. It wants to slam doors and run free. But your conscious mind is terrified of that savage inner fury. Good people are not supposed to harbor bitter rage or selfish tantrums. If that dark steam reaches the surface, your self-image as a kind, loving adult would shatter.\n\nTo prevent that emotional explosion, your brain hits the emergency override. It quickly orders the blood vessels in your neck, back, or gut to tighten up. Oxygen drops by a tiny fraction. Instantly, a bolt of agonizing physical pain strikes. You drop to the couch, clutching your back in tears. The trick works perfectly. Instead of confronting your hidden rage, you spend the week visiting chiropractors and applying heating pads. Your good reputation remains intact, but your body pays the bill for your silent self-sacrifice.",
